    "narrative": "The marquee plays for Ghost Ride the WHIP are heavily anchored by high-ceiling outfielders capitalizing on favorable delivery speeds and batting orders. **James Wood** (76.28) is our premier start today, buoyed by an elite 16% park boost and a matchup against a pitcher with a slow delivery, which maximizes his aggressive base-stealing potential. Similarly, **Mickey Moniak** (75.98) offers immense value from the two-hole, leveraging a massive 14% platoon edge against Bradley to push his efficiency well into the elite tier. While **Corbin Carroll** (74.38) and **Jose Caballero** (71.07) are dealing with high-caliber opposing arms, their profiles remain locked in; **Jose Caballero** specifically benefits from a 13% dynamic platoon boost that outweighs the minor risk posed by the opposing catcher\u2019s efficiency.\n\nIn the infield, **Brice Turang** (65.69) stands out as a high-floor selection, benefiting significantly from his elevated spot in the order and a favorable SP matchup that allows him to exploit the basepaths despite Rea's modest hold profile. The most contentious bench decision today involved **Alex Freeland**, who posted a 47.22. While his switch-hitting versatility is an asset, the optimizer prioritized the higher projected efficiency and specific park advantages of the starting nine, ultimately relegating **Alex Freeland** to the pine to ensure we don't bleed plate appearances on a marginal bat. We are also keeping a close eye on **Austin Riley** (49.16), whose score is suppressed by a tough matchup against McDonald; while he remains a starter, his proximity to the floor means he is a primary candidate for a mid-game swap if his early at-bats prove stagnant. \n\nNo major weather-related postponements loom, but we are exercising caution regarding **Dalton Rushing** (52.38) and **Josh Naylor** (58.45) as they face starters with xERAs in the low 4.00s. **Seiya Suzuki** remains on the bench (44.57) due to a difficult draw against Misiorowski, confirming our commitment to chasing favorable platoon splits over raw talent when the matchup dictates a lower probability of success. With **Trevor Story** still sidelined on the IL, the team remains focused on maximizing our daily cap efficiency to bridge the gap in our 5x5 categories.",
